# Generated files that flow one way: sources of truth -> document directory.
#
# `merge=ours` keeps OUR version when a merge brings in someone else's edit to
# a generated file — a coauthor working in Overleaf, or a `make template-update`
# from the upstream template. These files are rebuilt by `make sync-bib`,
# `make plots` and `make tables`, so a merge could only ever corrupt them.
#
# New bibliography entries a coauthor adds on Overleaf are NOT lost by this:
# `tools/bib_absorb.py` lifts them into the root references.bib before the copy
# is regenerated. See AGENTS.md, "Overleaf".
#
# `merge=ours` needs a driver by that name to exist in each clone. It cannot be
# committed; bootstrap.sh sets it, or run once:  git config merge.ours.driver true
docs/*/references.bib       merge=ours
docs/*/main.tex             merge=ours
docs/*/.template.yml        merge=ours
docs/*/*.sty                merge=ours
docs/*/tables/*.tex         merge=ours
docs/*/figures/*.pdf        binary merge=ours
docs/*/figures/*.png        binary merge=ours
docs/*/orcid.pdf            binary merge=ours
figures/generated/*.pdf     binary merge=ours
figures/generated/*.png     binary merge=ours

# Binary assets: never try to diff or merge these line-wise.
*.pdf                 binary
*.png                 binary
figures/generated/*.drawio.svg  -diff

# The W&B cache is CSV rather than Parquet precisely so that it diffs: a pull
# request that changes the paper's numbers shows the change as reviewable text.
# Normalize its endings like any other tracked text file.
*.csv   text eol=lf
*.tex   text eol=lf
*.bib   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
